;; I need to sort out how strings are organised.
;; Characters:
;; UTF-8 at the bottom?
;; UTF-16 organised into trees with prefix compression?
;;
;; Text is a tree. Units are organised into characters, characters into (most of
;; the time) words or syntactic units (lexical tokens) -> sentences/expressions
;; -> paragraphs/top level forms -> namespaces/documents -> packages/whatever.
;;
;; This isn't meant to convey any connection between text and code, just that
;; both are heirarchical and both can benefit from the right choice of
;; datastructure.
;;
;; So what does a rope look like? Something like crockford's kim, maybe.
;;
;; What if we start with a string as a sequence of sequences of bytes, each
;; subsequnce representing a code unit. If a number of units share a prefix,
;; they can be grouped into a subsequence of shorter units with the shared
;; prefix implied.
;;
;; Then ascii and basic mlp text will amortise to one and two bytes per
;; character respectively. But at the cost of indirection overhead.

;; How often will we indirect? Well, code in xprl that's constantly using μ and
;; other latex style symbols might be a problem. But we might choose never to
;; indirect if that's the case. Or we may choose to do a dynamic reencoding
;; huffman style.
;;
;; Most documents use a small subset of the full space of possible characters.
;; But we can't assume that those subsets overlap in any useful way with the
;; preassigned subspaces which are more about language purity.
;;
;; So what the hell does this look like more concretely? I don't know. I have a
;; lot of reading to do.
